## Provider lifecycle

**Terms used:** {% if tool_ui_kind == "in-app" %}[*Browser session*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#browser-session), [*In-app tool*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#in-app-tool), [*Provider blueprint*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#provider-blueprint), [*Tool frontend SDK*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#tool-frontend-sdk), [*Tool host*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#tool-host){% else %}[*Browser session*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#browser-session), [*Launch code*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#launch-code), [*Launch code exchange*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#launch-code-exchange), [*Provider blueprint*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#provider-blueprint), [*Standalone tool*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#standalone-tool), [*Tool access token*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#tool-access-token), [*Tool launch grant*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#tool-launch-grant), [*Trusted origin*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#trusted-origin){% endif %}.

`{{ provider_class }}` is instantiated without arguments when the API starts.
Its metadata is displayed in the Tools catalog.
`is_available` can hide it from individual users.
{% if tool_ui_kind == "standalone" %}
`launch` returns an absolute standalone URL.
The included blueprint serves the local page and *launch code exchange*. A provider can
set `blueprint = None` only when its external application needs no
datalab-hosted callback. An external application that exchanges *launch codes* through
datalab needs a service-authenticated *provider blueprint*.

The included provider issues a single-use *tool launch grant*.
The framework automatically requires an active datalab *browser session* and
*trusted origin* for every route in the blueprint. The exchange consumes the
grant and returns a *tool access token* to the standalone page.
The *launch code* is carried in the URL fragment, which is not sent with the
initial HTTP request, and the page removes it before making API requests.
The page keeps that token in memory and uses it as `DATALAB-API-KEY`; it does not
use the Flask session for datalab API calls and never handles a *permanent API key*.

For a separately hosted UI, expose any datalab-hosted exchange route through a
*provider blueprint* with `route_auth = ToolRouteAuth.SERVICE`, and implement
`authenticate_service_request()` for its machine client before calling
`exchange_launch_code`.
Keep the machine-client secret outside source control and never expose it to
browser JavaScript.
Return the *tool access token* only to an isolated user runtime or keep it in the
remote backend.
Never call `/get-api-key/` or ask users to paste a permanent key.
{% else %}
`launch` authorizes entry into the generic *datalab tool* host and returns no
external URL. The webapp then loads `frontend/tool.js` from this provider's
blueprint namespace and mounts the registered component below the normal
navigation.

The starter frontend:

- builds one classic IIFE JavaScript bundle;
- externalizes `vue` and uses the exact Vue runtime supplied by datalab;
- embeds its prefixed CSS into that bundle;
- synchronously registers through `window.DatalabToolSDK`; and
- accesses datalab through the SDK with the current *browser session*.

SDK version 1 exposes the host Vue runtime, a curated component set, `get` and
`post` API helpers, navigation, and dialogs. Treat that SDK as the public
frontend interface. Do not import datalab's private `@/` modules, Vuex store, or
source components directly, and do not bundle another copy of Vue. Avoid
dynamic imports because the host loads only the declared classic entrypoint.

`ToolView.vue` calls existing datalab API routes through the SDK, so the normal
current-user permission filters apply. The *browser session* is sufficient for
*in-app tools*: do not issue, store, or expose a *tool access token* in frontend
JavaScript.
{% endif %}

## Trust boundary

**Terms used:** [*Authentication*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#authentication), [*Item*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#item), [*Object-level authorization*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#object-level-authorization), [*Route authentication*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#route-authentication), [*Tool context*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#tool-context), [*Tool plugin*](https://docs.datalab-org.io/tools-glossary/#tool-plugin).

*Tool plugins* execute in the datalab API process and are
administrator-trusted code.
{% if tool_ui_kind == "in-app" %}
This plugin's compiled JavaScript also executes inside the authenticated
datalab web application. It can act with each signed-in user's session, inspect
or alter the page, and access every datalab resource that user may access.
{% endif %}
`ToolContext` is the supported interface for current-user metadata, but it is not a sandbox.
Prefer normal datalab API calls over importing database objects from the server package.
`is_available` controls catalog and launch visibility only.
Every declared blueprint route is authenticated automatically using the
provider's single route policy. Browser *authentication* does not apply *item*
permission filters to arbitrary database queries: custom backend routes must
still use permission-aware datalab services or `get_default_permissions()`.
Direct app-level route registration is unsupported.

The deployment administrator—not the registry or SDK validator—is responsible
for deciding whether this plugin is trustworthy.
{% if tool_ui_kind == "in-app" %}
Before installation and every upgrade, review its maintainers, Python and
JavaScript source, dependencies and lockfiles, release provenance, and compiled
browser bundle.
{% else %}
Before installation and every upgrade, review its maintainers, Python and
standalone frontend source, dependencies and lockfiles, release provenance, and
distributed package.
If the standalone UI or its backend is hosted separately, also review and trust
the remote operator, deployment, *tool access token* destination, and data retention and
deletion policies.
{% endif %}
